The core disagreement turns on whether B2 remains a legal reply for Black after White B1. The {C1,C2} stones start with exactly the two liberties B1/B2. White B1 reduces them to one. However, Black’s answer at B2 simultaneously connects those stones to the already-linked chain A2-B3-B4. The resulting single group now counts A1 (adjacent to A2) as a liberty; therefore B2 is not suicide and is a legal move. Once the connection is made, the combined shape acquires additional external liberties along the D-file and through C3/C4, preventing any immediate capture sequence.  

No other first-move candidate (B2, A3, C5, etc.) reduces every Black group to a single liberty without permitting an immediate connecting or capturing reply. Consequently no single White move initiates an unconditional kill against optimal Black play.

