10.text:
The split in round 3 rests on a single rules question: after White B1, is Black B2 suicide or a legal connection? Standard liberty counting resolves it unambiguously. B2's four orthogonal neighbors are A2/B3/C2 (Black) and B1 (White). Placement therefore gives the new stone zero liberties of its own. It also captures nothing, because the adjacent White stone on B1 retains its external liberty at A1. The move is therefore illegal suicide under the ko/suicide rule, regardless of any larger connected component that would exist if B2 could be played.  

Consequently B1 is a forcing atari that leaves Black with no legal reply at the remaining liberty; White captures on the next turn irrespective of Black's play elsewhere. No other empty intersection simultaneously reduces every relevant Black chain to a single liberty while denying any safe connecting or capturing reply. The correct set of forcing first moves is therefore {B1}.

